module coerce_pair.neg_1

// ONE seeded cause: TWO declared pairs from the same source type to DIFFERENT
// targets make two candidates applicable, and D429 R5 refuses rather than
// ranking them. There is no rule in the language that would prefer `Bytes` over
// `Text`, and inventing one at the call site is how a program starts depending
// on which declaration was read first.
//
// AGREEMENT, measured 2026-08-23: the oracle refuses this shape too -- it says
// "no matching overload for `take(nova_str)`" and lists both candidates, which
// is the same OUTCOME by a different route (it does not attempt a pair against
// an overload set at all). A fixture pair asserts the outcome, so this one is
// legal; novac's message names the RULE instead of the candidate list, and that
// is deliberate -- D420 wants the cause, and "no matching overload" sends the
// reader to look for a missing declaration that is right here.
type Bytes { n int }

type Text { n int }

#coerce fn str consume @to_bytes() -> Bytes => { n: 1 }

#coerce fn str consume @to_text() -> Text => { n: 2 }

fn take(b Bytes) -> int => b.n

fn take(t Text) -> int => t.n

fn main() {
    println(take("s"))
}
